ere $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=3{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=3\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int 3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =3x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{3x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332695/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77ba8502-9ddb-52d0-a885-33190dc04d4f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332695"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332727/selection/501005c36a1e9846d3d572d1e98556b02a78445cd10493627d8513e33756bf5a" :answer/id #uuid "da0d808e-dc9d-50e8-bff4-1a6b4be299e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A{e}^{5x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332727/selection/1f0ccb8cda48b997952b3f9299316a1452a4e0f17dc9369f19026796970d39ae" :answer/id #uuid "19175f7e-cbbe-5801-a551-fc2cb2144b1f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332727/selection/35f6a2f3c443561a1b0ed8d28dac0ec3a4388e7b2b0f1c6d7208dbf49f4dfce7" :answer/id #uuid "a7790186-798a-55f2-9df1-f054b2bfc006" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A{e}^{10x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332727/selection/e1897644270674f5833f995ab06c57b01aebbc3c686c078b5f97eb695d49fd2e" :answer/id #uuid "9a6cb816-72ba-5678-91a0-7b6966b4d89d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A{e}^{-5x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332727/selection/1a3a4598cf19e4b3839d66ed6010d31473fab8491428787c986aa2ee1009afd5" :answer/id #uuid "55d61e17-d082-5965-902b-da7949155288" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A{e}^{-10x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332727/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4a13dc07-ef86-5938-9018-5339d4e4f54c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-332727/selection/501005c36a1e9846d3d572d1e98556b02a78445cd10493627d8513e33756bf5a" :answer-field/choices ["q-332727/selection/501005c36a1e9846d3d572d1e98556b02a78445cd10493627d8513e33756bf5a" "q-332727/selection/1f0ccb8cda48b997952b3f9299316a1452a4e0f17dc9369f19026796970d39ae" "q-332727/selection/35f6a2f3c443561a1b0ed8d28dac0ec3a4388e7b2b0f1c6d7208dbf49f4dfce7" "q-332727/selection/e1897644270674f5833f995ab06c57b01aebbc3c686c078b5f97eb695d49fd2e" "q-332727/selection/1a3a4598cf19e4b3839d66ed6010d31473fab8491428787c986aa2ee1009afd5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332727" :question/id #uuid "b7704c4e-4d81-520a-bf5d-625b60f19113" :question/math-academy-id "q-332727" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=10.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=5{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=5\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 5\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int 5\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =5x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{5x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{5x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332727/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77ba8502-9ddb-52d0-a885-33190dc04d4f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332727"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332689/selection/0bf48b8ef873df18a23a93510256f82fc38db7145b11309a9899dafdd7ff95ae" :answer/id #uuid "2d505866-b0ce-59dd-bace-80238eff0e44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A{e}^{-6x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332689/selection/7e0716ee44c4440b56499b93cfa0fd82154bb47dec80337819c9a11e6734f41b" :answer/id #uuid "b9b341c2-9972-5655-b05b-6c4eaadfd4e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A{e}^{-3x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332689/selection/273cf6c29e8963346e69ff835b6c3454527d0b9ce8ff0c226c8f8b1be0f49008" :answer/id #uuid "1bb0315a-d8ef-5718-a80a-0ac178eb8c72" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A{e}^{6x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332689/selection/81b0abd37faec18752dd0680dc5d8749459665100cfde64af75643f93934485e" :answer/id #uuid "6cf62c87-31cb-578b-adeb-682c68542e9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A{e}^{3x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332689/selection/da2f141193026d1f707910d9097f6b5b7bcde53ca609172d98684ffb6b3bdda4" :answer/id #uuid "56333813-3a40-58f7-bb79-b66602804e9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332689/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c0cb2419-d08b-5892-9362-899f035eaa5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-332689/selection/7e0716ee44c4440b56499b93cfa0fd82154bb47dec80337819c9a11e6734f41b" :answer-field/choices ["q-332689/selection/0bf48b8ef873df18a23a93510256f82fc38db7145b11309a9899dafdd7ff95ae" "q-332689/selection/7e0716ee44c4440b56499b93cfa0fd82154bb47dec80337819c9a11e6734f41b" "q-332689/selection/273cf6c29e8963346e69ff835b6c3454527d0b9ce8ff0c226c8f8b1be0f49008" "q-332689/selection/81b0abd37faec18752dd0680dc5d8749459665100cfde64af75643f93934485e" "q-332689/selection/da2f141193026d1f707910d9097f6b5b7bcde53ca609172d98684ffb6b3bdda4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332689" :question/id #uuid "f1bead44-c2c5-5957-95b7-3602579f7a1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-332689" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=-4{x}^{2}+x-6.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-3{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-3\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int -3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =-3x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{-3x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332689/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77ba8502-9ddb-52d0-a885-33190dc04d4f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332689"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332729/selection/2a63dfcc87c5a8d49816fb12c6f35925a87c6ed4e45646902e2043620aa1f905" :answer/id #uuid "263fbeda-62bb-5af0-91d6-a27e1998a862" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A{e}^{8x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332729/selection/ff178e0cc2a2b7b20ff2eca53c6bed5ba0f812685f7779924e74da8674d27c4b" :answer/id #uuid "2406394a-8997-54dc-b3a1-a917aa19f207" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A{e}^{-8x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332729/selection/39c004a25915781a6897602005bd3c868d4057e2925463856ceb775d935cf10b" :answer/id #uuid "1252bcdb-b25d-5f52-9151-33b84ef065ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A{e}^{4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332729/selection/1d58f4ad044acb6156300b92774f2ab5c577de50c5c44d4ace0202cf215279cf" :answer/id #uuid "f48d9b4f-4a1c-5d90-8a61-b8d9adec1aa9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332729/selection/bee8c6ba40c00c089738a3825830a5665740093ef771fbbfac5dd805221ece2e" :answer/id #uuid "044f515a-bab6-5417-87b3-7d470407faca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A{e}^{-4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332729/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "650c0d7c-77e1-50be-8060-872df5db7aaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-332729/selection/bee8c6ba40c00c089738a3825830a5665740093ef771fbbfac5dd805221ece2e" :answer-field/choices ["q-332729/selection/2a63dfcc87c5a8d49816fb12c6f35925a87c6ed4e45646902e2043620aa1f905" "q-332729/selection/ff178e0cc2a2b7b20ff2eca53c6bed5ba0f812685f7779924e74da8674d27c4b" "q-332729/selection/39c004a25915781a6897602005bd3c868d4057e2925463856ceb775d935cf10b" "q-332729/selection/1d58f4ad044acb6156300b92774f2ab5c577de50c5c44d4ace0202cf215279cf" "q-332729/selection/bee8c6ba40c00c089738a3825830a5665740093ef771fbbfac5dd805221ece2e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332729" :question/id #uuid "c2f8e17b-55ad-5eab-aacc-5137772c2aac" :question/math-academy-id "q-332729" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=-6.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-4{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-4\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int -4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =-4x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{-4x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{-4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332729/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77ba8502-9ddb-52d0-a885-33190dc04d4f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332729"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332305/field-1/eb941993c12de92cf2de354bb5e8224ce83fb9350fc9526828b4473e449796f5" :answer/id #uuid "e3b96e4e-7fa1-5f99-807a-a658874ee80f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332305/field-1/186e415469c1cda52c815710b2cebac0467cf7fe4ab401af767ee19afe159e8e" :answer/id #uuid "00e7cf25-dbbe-5487-a493-9e7445060fbd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332305/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "1ec864c8-9080-546f-9ff9-8532c22a57a5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332305/field-1/eb941993c12de92cf2de354bb5e8224ce83fb9350fc9526828b4473e449796f5" :answer-field/choices ["q-332305/field-1/eb941993c12de92cf2de354bb5e8224ce83fb9350fc9526828b4473e449796f5" "q-332305/field-1/186e415469c1cda52c815710b2cebac0467cf7fe4ab401af767ee19afe159e8e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332305" :question/id #uuid "c13ccfab-5d20-52e4-a53a-32d4c8c68460" :question/math-academy-id "q-332305" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y=-4x-7.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha -4(\\alpha x+\\beta )=-4x-7\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-4\\alpha x+(\\alpha -4\\beta )=-4x-7.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -4\\beta =-7\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\,\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x+2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-332305/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf16afad-30ed-5055-a86c-28affd3d8930"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332305"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332323/selection/5fb93ed9c7830dc7d2068f42e7f70346db917589113ebba11a94920c87fcfb77" :answer/id #uuid "e1a571e7-3b74-5ab5-9e2c-0883e761ede7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{x}^{2}-2x-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332323/selection/8bc7f9668d4136848320895724a1096d3bc857fc25f870011e8a7dc605c3d261" :answer/id #uuid "257b8da1-ffdf-555e-b5a2-1057d77055ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{x}^{2}+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332323/selection/a85355616bf2f422186198eba8dd254d37d3dd32578f64db08b53e982fab0d45" :answer/id #uuid "2104400c-db5a-5566-bb1c-c92b5fcd4b60" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{x}^{2}-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332323/selection/86543def3248a83740ed6e39d830adafced4be0a198651b6514125bafcebea63" :answer/id #uuid "799dfbc0-6a59-5101-b879-6c04e18769f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332323/selection/fed108602a926a6a0bf7f57777003559b6a87c2de5c8125f54b781e223459d39" :answer/id #uuid "b69b0cab-6362-5ce6-b6f7-31feaafd6c03" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5{x}^{2}-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332323/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1532b375-6425-5c93-b0e3-ee7b369f92a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-332323/selection/a85355616bf2f422186198eba8dd254d37d3dd32578f64db08b53e982fab0d45" :answer-field/choices ["q-332323/selection/5fb93ed9c7830dc7d2068f42e7f70346db917589113ebba11a94920c87fcfb77" "q-332323/selection/8bc7f9668d4136848320895724a1096d3bc857fc25f870011e8a7dc605c3d261" "q-332323/selection/a85355616bf2f422186198eba8dd254d37d3dd32578f64db08b53e982fab0d45" "q-332323/selection/86543def3248a83740ed6e39d830adafced4be0a198651b6514125bafcebea63" "q-332323/selection/fed108602a926a6a0bf7f57777003559b6a87c2de5c8125f54b781e223459d39"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332323" :question/id #uuid "776d5ca9-c850-5a1e-a975-705e11432c48" :question/math-academy-id "q-332323" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-{x}^{2}-2x-2.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-2x-2\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha +\\beta )x+(\\beta +\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-2x-2.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\beta +\\gamma =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =0,\\gamma =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}-2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-332323/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf16afad-30ed-5055-a86c-28affd3d8930"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332323"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332325/selection/86543def3248a83740ed6e39d830adafced4be0a198651b6514125bafcebea63" :answer/id #uuid "4fdd41e7-1716-5080-880c-8d77427fb67b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332325/selection/7de6f2cc2c76d94ac20decace7b48c7a3c02a8868995317fd8297a5d0545d4e8" :answer/id #uuid "081d6a19-0fb7-5c09-9804-eceae088cdf1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332325/selection/1e9028fe9a462527409e3797c08c5613692177d9aa363cc05ccd462630399bb3" :answer/id #uuid "85db2297-1a5f-5d99-9781-6143224694a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332325/selection/0d517a82d38807827e78ec994c24cb58d44711f4153cbb6f91f6118370ed8b3c" :answer/id #uuid "ea06e80a-8d93-58cc-ba4f-64e85b08492d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332325/selection/d2907e3e99682e7af265bbcb0ec2705b439bae684cf06212f6e9bb73c90d18bd" :answer/id #uuid "10a365a2-a606-5373-89a8-8d9d4b2c0b4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332325/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c82706d3-e7dd-5e98-b527-d5533e439c95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-332325/selection/0d517a82d38807827e78ec994c24cb58d44711f4153cbb6f91f6118370ed8b3c" :answer-field/choices ["q-332325/selection/86543def3248a83740ed6e39d830adafced4be0a198651b6514125bafcebea63" "q-332325/selection/7de6f2cc2c76d94ac20decace7b48c7a3c02a8868995317fd8297a5d0545d4e8" "q-332325/selection/1e9028fe9a462527409e3797c08c5613692177d9aa363cc05ccd462630399bb3" "q-332325/selection/0d517a82d38807827e78ec994c24cb58d44711f4153cbb6f91f6118370ed8b3c" "q-332325/selection/d2907e3e99682e7af265bbcb0ec2705b439bae684cf06212f6e9bb73c90d18bd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332325" :question/id #uuid "2af28a4b-d570-53bf-8474-03cfab3d34eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-332325" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y={x}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )={x}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(4\\alpha +2\\beta )x+(2\\beta +2\\gamma )={x}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 4\\alpha +2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\beta +2\\gamma =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =-1,\\gamma =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-332325/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf16afad-30ed-5055-a86c-28affd3d8930"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332325"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332546/field-1/7fd6106cd421ffa346551615508e568958b9f41e5c1f045a30552f9bedeb10c6" :answer/id #uuid "e0db1856-65f8-5ee2-a470-df2611fe28cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Ae^{-3x}-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332546/field-1/fa7a5f341a297350cb9c2816316f0f22b83da7722a13f5ae0f5a919e130ff31e" :answer/id #uuid "54d4340c-4a94-5c86-9107-747b9d65dfca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Ae^{-3x}+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332546/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "990c8afb-7e39-535b-a607-326439100b8e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332546/field-1/7fd6106cd421ffa346551615508e568958b9f41e5c1f045a30552f9bedeb10c6" :answer-field/choices ["q-332546/field-1/7fd6106cd421ffa346551615508e568958b9f41e5c1f045a30552f9bedeb10c6" "q-332546/field-1/fa7a5f341a297350cb9c2816316f0f22b83da7722a13f5ae0f5a919e130ff31e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332546" :question/id #uuid "98b14171-a190-5722-ab9c-1b6df5d59394" :question/math-academy-id "q-332546" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=-9.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+3\\beta =-9\n$$\n\nSolving this equation gives $\\beta =-3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}-3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332546/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332546"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332272/field-1/be01a35e88c3b00ba1501716ac279c6111870f01fb710c7af1995a1b28707767" :answer/id #uuid "e51308c7-7de6-5d87-917d-110f1f0ce9ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e^{4x}-2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186228013 :answer-field/choices ["q-332272/field-1/be01a35e88c3b00ba1501716ac279c6111870f01fb710c7af1995a1b28707767"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-332272"] :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=8x-4,\\,y(0)=\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=8x-4\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha -4(\\alpha x+\\beta )=8x-4\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-4\\alpha x+(\\alpha -4\\beta )=8x-4\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -4\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\,\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}-2x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=\\frac{3}{2}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{4(0)}-2(0)+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ A+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{4x}-2x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-333050/selection/3c5a212c6d74e4da1dadc8e2b991f99d123206126b257761a6182cf5b79b9081" :answer/id #uuid "9af41d08-91fd-5821-a7b9-e6bbdf8a5f02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{7}{2}{e}^{2x}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{9}{2}x-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333050/selection/c3732e639e125ec8e700643e45b575cceb5c3d11aafd5fa6c8e8479983ab2cf1" :answer/id #uuid "3c1d8db7-bf1d-5047-a846-86b7132152b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{2x}+\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{9}{2}x+\\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333050/selection/50e3509567a71fc5c4e97688c3dda1a44d432c2cb79c3227e7d21de2b1f2eb97" :answer/id #uuid "8020fc8c-de2d-5181-a8ed-5d50d12bd107" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{9}{2}x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333050/selection/95f5b27b24bcc46a1cc2556ace03f866fb1544647e0efb636724d3275a67bb40" :answer/id #uuid "b52a7020-6da2-5f2f-81db-5adb4b15b3d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{9}{4}{e}^{2x}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{9}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333050/selection/4a6e7add765874bcf6dfdc6686500d695c96cd1c6f91ee843145c20f77ccef98" :answer/id #uuid "2ed7fa30-2d49-5a7d-a764-a06da3f9f397" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{9}{2}x+\\frac{7}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333050/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7dddebda-3da1-57bb-b056-a70cd1561e15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-333050/selection/4a6e7add765874bcf6dfdc6686500d695c96cd1c6f91ee843145c20f77ccef98" :answer-field/choices ["q-333050/selection/3c5a212c6d74e4da1dadc8e2b991f99d123206126b257761a6182cf5b79b9081" "q-333050/selection/c3732e639e125ec8e700643e45b575cceb5c3d11aafd5fa6c8e8479983ab2cf1" "q-333050/selection/50e3509567a71fc5c4e97688c3dda1a44d432c2cb79c3227e7d21de2b1f2eb97" "q-333050/selection/95f5b27b24bcc46a1cc2556ace03f866fb1544647e0efb636724d3275a67bb40" "q-333050/selection/4a6e7add765874bcf6dfdc6686500d695c96cd1c6f91ee843145c20f77ccef98"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333050" :question/id #uuid "bf750bb0-666e-5f39-a259-8e815751596f" :question/math-academy-id "q-333050" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=5{x}^{2}+4x-8,\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=5{x}^{2}+4x-8\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x},$ solve the initial value problem." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=5{x}^{2}+4x-8\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha -2\\beta )x+(\\beta -2\\gamma )=5{x}^{2}+4x-8\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha =5\\,(\\text{equating the coefficients of }{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha -2\\beta =4\\,(\\text{equating the coefficients of }x) \\\\ \\beta -2\\gamma =-8\\,(\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{5}{2},$ $\\beta =-\\frac{9}{2},$ and $\\gamma =\\frac{7}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{9}{2}x+\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{9}{2}x+\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial condition.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{2(0)}-\\frac{5}{2}(0)^{2}-\\frac{9}{2}(0)+\\frac{7}{4} & =2 \\\\ A+\\frac{7}{4} & =2 \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{9}{2}x+\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-333050/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34158a33-c6b6-55dc-a4a7-2b5c1c753ac6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333050"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332558/field-1/c40984310bba2a31f0ef04b60ac520ae5d0ec3cff19320435aad4ae71008ecc9" :answer/id #uuid "2af80dc5-e39b-5b4e-b9ac-64f543786310" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e^{-2x}+2x+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332558/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "2f6274f7-31a7-531a-821e-f3a1796a96d7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332558/field-1/c40984310bba2a31f0ef04b60ac520ae5d0ec3cff19320435aad4ae71008ecc9" :answer-field/choices ["q-332558/field-1/c40984310bba2a31f0ef04b60ac520ae5d0ec3cff19320435aad4ae71008ecc9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332558" :question/id #uuid "e09ee532-dc3c-5770-ae00-babef0c316b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-332558" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=4x+6,\\,y(0)=3.\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=4x+6\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +2(\\alpha x+\\beta )=4x+6\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha x+(\\alpha +2\\beta )=4x+6\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +2\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\,\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2x+2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+2x+2.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=3$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-2(0)}+2(0)+2 & =3 \\\\ A+2 & =3 \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-2x}+2x+2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-332558/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34158a33-c6b6-55dc-a4a7-2b5c1c753ac6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332558"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
